% The same evidence against the exception, but with the 0.58 on the
% PREMISE of a certain rule instead of on the rule. A confidence on a rule
% lowers the strength of what the rule derives; a confidence on a premise
% is the probability that the rule applies at all. Here: with probability
% 0.58 the rule derives a certain -injured(a) and the exception is
% cancelled completely; with probability 0.42 the rule does not apply and
% the exception keeps its full 0.6. flies(a) is reported with
% 0.58*1 + 0.42*(1-0.6) = 0.748, not the 0.98 of bird_counter.gkp.
bird(a).
0.6::injured(a).
0.58::vet(a).
-injured(X) :- vet(X).
flies(X) :- bird(X), unless(injured(X), 2).
query(flies(a)).
